---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatXmlJson from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatXmlJson.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="xml-to-json">
  <DevFormatXmlJson client:load direction="xml-to-json" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>There is no single correct XML to JSON mapping</h2>
    <p>
      XML and JSON have different data models. XML has attributes, ordered children of mixed types, mixed
      content, namespaces, comments and processing instructions. JSON has objects, arrays and four scalar
      types. Each converter makes its own choices, so the same XML file gives different JSON in different
      tools.
    </p>
    <p>
      This tool shows its choices, and tells you during conversion when something cannot be represented
      exactly.
    </p>

    <h2>The three decisions you control</h2>
    <h3>Attributes</h3>
    <p>
      <code>&lt;item id="1"&gt;Widget&lt;/item&gt;</code> has two pieces of data, and the attribute has no
      obvious place in JSON. Here attribute keys get a prefix, which gives
      <code>&#123;"item": &#123;"@id": "1", "#text": "Widget"&#125;&#125;</code>. Without a prefix, an attribute
      called <code>id</code> and a child element called <code>id</code> would collide, and one would be lost
      without warning. Change the prefix to <code>$</code>, <code>_</code> or what your consumer expects.
    </p>
    <h3>Single elements versus arrays</h3>
    <p>
      This causes production bugs. <code>&lt;items&gt;&lt;item/&gt;&lt;item/&gt;&lt;/items&gt;</code> becomes an
      array; <code>&lt;items&gt;&lt;item/&gt;&lt;/items&gt;</code> becomes a single object. Code that calls
      <code>items.item.map(…)</code> works for a week and crashes on the day a customer has exactly one order.
      List element names in the <strong>always arrays</strong> field, and they are arrays whatever their
      count in the document.
    </p>
    <h3>Scalar coercion</h3>
    <p>
      XML has no types: every attribute and text node is a string. Turning <code>"42"</code> into
      <code>42</code> is convenient but wrong for values that only look numeric: postcodes, phone numbers,
      version strings, account numbers with leading zeros, ISBNs. So coercion is off by default. Turn it on
      only when you know the shape of your data.
    </p>

    <h2>What JSON cannot represent</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>XML feature</th><th>In JSON</th><th>Consequence</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Mixed content</td><td>Text collected into one key</td><td>The position of the text relative to sibling elements is lost</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Order between differently-named siblings</td><td>Object keys</td><td>Grouped by name; the original interleaving is gone</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Namespaces</td><td>Prefixed names or stripped</td><td>Two elements from different namespaces with the same local name collide if you strip</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Comments</td><td>Dropped, or a <code>#comment</code> key</td><td>Off by default</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Processing instructions and DOCTYPE</td><td>Dropped</td><td>Not representable</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CDATA</td><td>Ordinary text</td><td>Correct: CDATA is lexical, not semantic</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>When your document has mixed content or interleaved siblings, a note under the output names the problem.</p>

    <h2>Namespaces: strip or keep?</h2>
    <p>
      If you keep them, you get keys such as <code>"soap:Envelope"</code> and <code>"dc:title"</code>. They are
      awkward in JavaScript (you cannot write <code>obj.soap:Envelope</code>) but unambiguous. If you strip
      them, you get <code>"Envelope"</code> and <code>"title"</code>, which are easy to use until a document
      mixes two namespaces that both define <code>title</code>. Strip for a document you control; keep for
      documents from an external system.
    </p>

    <h2>The reverse direction</h2>
    <p>
      The swap button changes the tool to <a href="/json-to-xml">JSON to XML</a> and uses the current output as
      the new input, so you can check the round trip. The result is not byte-identical, because attribute
      order, whitespace and dropped comments change, but the data stays the same.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
